From def1114f07a2567d594d23e0bc26723752065ef0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yichao Yu <yyc1992@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:52:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 21/21] slsqp: solve the LDP without forming G*R^-1

LSI transformed the inequality constraints into those of a least
distance problem by forming G*R^-1 (one row per constraint) with DTRSM,
O(mg*n^2), and LDP copied it, transposed and extended by h, into the
(n+1 x mg) matrix of its NNLS dual.  NNLS then applied every Householder
reflection and Givens rotation to all mg columns and computed the dual
vector from all of them, two or three sweeps over the whole matrix per
outer iteration.

Keep the matrix implicit instead.  NNLS accumulates the orthogonal
transformation of the rows in an explicit (n+1 x n+1) matrix Q,
transforms a column (one triangular solve with R and one product with
Q) only when it is tested for entering the solution set, and keeps the
transformed columns of the solution set by position.  The dual vector
is computed from the residual in the original coordinates with one
triangular solve and one DGEMV with the original G, and LDP's solution
with one DGEMV and one triangular solve.  The time per NNLS outer
iteration is O(n^2 + mg*n) and the LDP memory O(n^2 + mg) instead of
O(mg*n).  LSI no longer modifies G, so LSEI no longer copies it either.
The buffer size is rederived accordingly.

LSI also returns mode 5 (A not of rank n) when A has fewer rows than
columns; the original code assumed ma >= n without checking (it always
holds in SLSQP, where A is the square factor of the BFGS matrix).

The iterates are those of the original algorithm up to rounding.  On
random LSEI problems the solutions agree to ~1e-9 (multipliers differ
only where they are not unique), and on degenerate ones, where NNLS
works at the rounding level, either version occasionally reports
incompatible constraints when the other does not, about equally often.
On a beam-spectrum problem with 200-320 variables and 400-3600
constraints this cuts the solver time per evaluation by 2.4-5.9x with
the built-in routines and 1.6-3.1x with OpenBLAS (three machines), with
the same optima; evaluation counts can differ slightly.  With 800
variables and 1600 linear constraints the gain is 11-12x and 3.2-5.9x.
With few constraints (5-25, or 10 with 200-800 variables) the time is
unchanged within the noise of the measurement (0.87-1.62x, no
consistent slowdown).

