+The least distance problem of LSI is solved without forming the
+transformed constraint matrix G*R^-1 (one row per inequality constraint)
+or the transposed copy of it that NNLS works on.  NNLS keeps the
+orthogonal transformation of the rows as an explicit (n+1) x (n+1)
+matrix, transforms the column of a constraint only when it is considered
+for the solution set, and computes the dual vector from the residual
+with a triangular solve and one DGEMV with the original G.  This removes
+the O(m*n^2) formation and the updates of all m columns in every NNLS
+iteration, so that the time per NNLS iteration is O(n^2 + m*n) and the
+memory O(n^2 + m) instead of O(m*n), and LSI no longer modifies G.  The
+iterates are the same up to rounding, but results differ from SciPy's in
+the last bits and evaluation counts can differ slightly.
 
 The BLAS/LAPACK library is chosen at configure time with the CMake
 option NLOPT_BLAS: AUTO (default; use one if found), ON (require one),
